erson\nClient Portal\ncontract\nPay My Bill\nbadge\nStaff Directory\nvolunteer_activism\nGive\nYour Visit\nPlan your visit\nBook an appointment\nPrepare for your visit\nConstruction updates\nLocations\nOwner resources\nClient portal\nCounseling and support\nClient rights and responsibilities\nPay my bill\nPayment policy\nPharmacy and refills\nOur expertise\nAbout your care team\nAnimal Care\nFind a service by animal\nCats and dogs\nExotics\nEquine\nLivestock\nCare resources\nBook an appointment\nPharmacy and refills\nCounseling and support\nNews and health tips\nEmergency Care\nGet help now\nCall us 24/7\nDirections\nPrepare\nCare by animal\nCats and dogs\nExotics\nEquine\nLivestock\nDiagnostic Laboratories\nSubmit and order\nLearn about the laboratory\nFind a diagnostic test\nFind a form\nSubmit a sample\nCheck lab results\nPay my bill\nDiagnostic services\nAvian diagnostics\nBacteriology\nChemistry and toxicology\nClinical hematopathology\nClinical pathology\nHistology\nMolecular diagnostics\nParasitology\nPathology\nSpecialized infectious disease\nTransmissible spongiform encephalopathies\nVirology\nResearch services\nAnatomic Pathology Research Services\nClinical pathology\nMolecular Pathology Research Services\nNext-Generation Sequencing Core\nColorado Avian Health Program\nInnovate and Learn\nResearch and trials\nClinical trials\nNews\nPrograms and training\nContinuing education\nResidencies\nInternships and externships\nTools and resources\nGuide to poisonous plants\nEquine recommended deworming schedule\nCanine fish oil dosing chart\nCanine pain scale\nFeline pain scale\nEquine pain scale\nAbout\nWho we are\nOur expertise\nFaculty and staff directory\nVeterinary Health Campus tour\nVolunteer\nConnect and support\nContact and locations\nCareers\nGive\nPolicies\nsearch\nSearch\nSearch\n×\nSearch for:\nSearch\nSkip to content\nClinical Trials\nImproving care through clinical trials\nClinical trials help doctors in the medical and veterinary fields discover new ways to improve the detection, diagnosis, ​and treatment of illnesses, all while enhancing the quality of care each patient receives. Clinical trials are critical to our groundbreaking research, may provide access to cutting-edge care, and result in discoveries that help people and animals in the future, all while providing your pet with the highest level of personalized care.\nbiotech\nParticipate in a clinical trial\nWhen your pet participates in a clinical trial, they help our veterinarians discover disease prevention methods and find new ways to improve detection, diagnosis, and treatment of animal illnesses.\nSearch open trials\narrow_forward\nQuestions?\nContact the\nVeterinary Clinical Trials Office\nat\n[email protected]\n.\nFrequently asked questions\nWhy should my pet participate?\n+\nClinical trials help us evaluate new diagnostics, treatments, or devices to better understand their safety and how well they work. We carefully design studies with strict ethical oversight and ensure you understand the trial’s purpose and any risks. Clinical trials often offer benefits, including access to cutting-edge treatments or financial incentives. All pets enrolled in clinical trials receive unparalleled care from their veterinary team, just like all other VHS patients.\nWhat should I expect during a clinical trial?\n+\nWhen you inquire about a clinical trial, the clinical trial team will review your pet’s medical history. If they appear to be a good fit, you may be contacted to move to the screening step, which may include a review of your veterinarian’s records and an in-person visit. You will have time to review the client informed consent documents and ask questions related to participation in the trial. You will also be presented with alternatives to participating in the clinical trial. You will learn about the study requirements, including any treatments and follow-up visits that are required. You can decide not to enroll in the trial or withdraw at any time without any negative impact on the care you and your pet will receive.\nAre clinical trials safe?\n+\nAll clinical trials undergo extensive review before starting, including review of any potential risks. Just as many aspects of veterinary care carry inherent risks or potential side effects, clinical trials may also have these. The care team will discuss any potential risks with you prior to enrollment.\nDoes it cost anything to participate in a clinical trial?\n+\nThere may be some associated costs to establish a diagnosis before clinical trial enrollment. Each study will have different associated costs and this will be outlined in the client informed consent document.\nHow many visits are required and how long does a trial last?\n+\nThis is variable depending on the study, ranging from a single visit to lifelong follow-up.\nHow do I know if my pet qualifies for a clinical trial?\n+\nEnrollment criteria are established for each clinical trial and may include factors such as size, breed, age, or diagnosis. These will vary for each clinical trial. Please inquire for an individual clinical trial to see if your pet may qualify.\nWill my primary veterinary be updated if I enroll my pet in a clinical trial?\n+\nYes, your care team will send updates to your veterinarian regarding the care your pet receives as a part of the clinical trial.\nClinical trials news\nCSU clinical trial studies ketamine therapy to help horses with chronic arthritis pain\nThe long-term effect of ketamine on horses with chronic pain has yet to be studied. Learn more about…\nSeptember 25, 2026\nMedical Advances\nAdvancing care for dogs with arthritis\nAfter being diagnosed with elbow arthritis, Daisy's vet recommended she participate in one of CSU’…\nMay 20, 2026\nMedical Advances\nDog days: The experience of a clinical trial for a dog and his family\nDog days: The experience of a clinical trial for a dog and his family.\nMarch 23, 2026\nPatient Stories\nDog aging project\nThe Dog Aging Project is a scientifically rigorous approach to understand the biology of aging in co…\nMarch 23, 2026\nMedical Advances\nMore stories\nVisit and Owner Resources\nBook an appointment\nHours and locations\nParking and directions\nPay my bill\nPharmacy and refills\nReferrals and Diagnostics\nRefer a patient\nSubmit a sample\nTest information and pricing\nShipping, drop off, and courier\nResults portal\nInnovate and Learn\nClinical trials\nContinuing education\nNews and health tips\nCollege of Veterinary Medicine and Biomedical Sciences\nAbout and Support\nOur expertise\nCareers\nGive\nDirectory\nPolicies\nEmployee resources\nContact Us\nMailing address\n300 W. Drake Rd.\nFort Collins, CO 80523\nContact and locations\nApply to CSU\nContact CSU\nDisclaimer\nEqual Opportunity\nPrivacy Statement\nAccessibility Statement\nCopyright © 2026 Colorado State University",
